      "text": "To determine the value of \\( n \\) that makes the seven-digit number \\( 854n526 \\) divisible by 11, we use the rule for divisibility by 11. According to this rule, a number is divisible by 11 if the difference between the sum of the digits in the odd positions and the sum of the digits in the even positions is a multiple of 11 (including 0).\n\nLet's identify the digits in the odd and even positions of the number \\( 854n526 \\):\n\n- The digits in the odd positions are: \\( 8, 4, 5, 6 \\)\n- The digits in the even positions are: \\( 5, n, 2 \\)\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the digits in the odd positions:\n\\[\n8 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 23\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the sum of the digits in the even positions:\n\\[\n5 + n + 2 = 7 + n\n\\]\n\nAccording to the divisibility rule for 11, the difference between these two sums must be a multiple of 11:\n\\[\n23 - (7 + n) = 16 - n\n\\]\n\nWe need \\( 16 - n \\) to be a multiple of 11. The possible multiples of 11 are \\( 0, \\pm 11, \\pm 22, \\ldots \\). Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\\[\n16 - n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\\[\n16 - n = 11k \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 16 - n \\) must be a non-negative multiple of 11, we consider the possible values:\n\\[\n16 - n = 0 \\implies n = 16\n\\]\n\\[\n16 - n = 11 \\implies n = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n16 - n = -11 \\implies n = 27 \\quad (\\text{not valid since } n \\text{ must be a digit})\n\\]\n\nWe also check \\( n = -5 \\), but this is not a valid digit. Therefore, the valid solutions are:\n\\[\nn = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad n = 16\n\\]\n\nSince \\( n \\) must be a single digit, the valid solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]",
